                <h2>Account Health Metrics: Preventing Campaign Pauses from Low Balance</h2>
<p>The primary goal of fund management is ensuring continuous ad delivery. When an account balance hits the warning threshold without timely top-up, ad sets pause and lose momentum—resulting in ROAS drops that often take weeks to recover.</p>
<p><strong>Actionable Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Set three-tier alert thresholds based on historical daily spend (Recommended: trigger notifications when balance covers 3/7/15 days of average consumption)</li>
<li>[ ] Enable real-time balance push notifications or API monitoring to prevent manual oversight</li>
<li>[ ] Separate primary accounts from testing accounts with different alert rules; testing accounts can have more relaxed thresholds</li>
<li>[ ] Account for top-up processing time: credit/debit cards are typically instant; wire transfers require 2-5 business days—plan your top-up schedule accordingly</li>
</ul>
<h2>Spend Efficiency Metrics: Every Dollar Should Work</h2>
<p>Idle funds sitting in accounts waste money, but excessive spending without conversions is equally wasteful. Spend efficiency metrics help teams evaluate whether budgets are being effectively utilized.</p>
<p><strong>Quick Reference Table:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Definition</th>
<th>Healthy Range</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Daily spend ratio</td>
<td>Budget consumption speed</td>
<td>80%-120% is normal; below 80% wastes impression opportunities</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPM volatility</td>
<td>Traffic competition stability</td>
<td>Week-over-week variance should not exceed 20%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA trend</td>
<td>Customer acquisition efficiency</td>
<td>Requires investigation if CPA exceeds target by 15% for 3+ consecutive days</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Actionable Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Review daily spend curves for smoothness—avoid situations where 80% of daily budget depletes by afternoon</li>
<li>[ ] When CPM drops more than 30%, check for platform traffic allocation changes or account weight decline</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor new ad group spend-to-conversion alignment for the first 3 days; pause underperformers promptly</li>
</ul>
<h2>Cash Flow Capacity: Top-up Rhythm Determines Scaling Ceiling</h2>
<p>Many teams fail to scale campaigns not because of poor creatives, but due to cash flow issues. Payment terms, top-up frequency, and currency exchange losses directly impact available budget capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Actionable Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Evaluate platform payment policies: Google Ads offers monthly invoicing; Meta enterprise accounts support NET-15/NET-30 terms; TikTok typically requires prepaid balance</li>
<li>[ ] Calculate optimal top-up cycles: too frequent increases fees and administrative burden; too infrequent may trigger balance alerts</li>
<li>[ ] Reserve a 10%-15% buffer for currency fluctuation, especially during volatile months</li>
<li>[ ] Compare platform official rates with channel rates before large top-ups; investigate further if the difference exceeds 1%</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Teams using multi-platform management tools should set up a unified fund dashboard to avoid information delays from checking each platform separately.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Compliance &amp; Risk Control Metrics: Protecting Your Financial Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Overseas advertising platforms enforce strict compliance requirements. Policy violations leading to account suspension may result in frozen account balances that cannot be withdrawn. Risk control metrics help teams identify issues before they escalate.</p>
<p><strong>Actionable Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Regularly check account policy compliance status: Meta Business Manager and Google Ads MCC both provide violation alert portals</li>
<li>[ ] Set single-account daily spend caps to prevent malicious clicks or system anomalies causing abnormal spending</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor Invalid Traffic (IVT) reports—investigate and file platform claims if IVT exceeds 3%</li>
<li>[ ] Ensure ad content complies with target market regulations (e.g., EU GDPR, US FTC guidelines) to prevent account restrictions</li>
<li>[ ] Retain all top-up records, ad spend reports, and financial reconciliation documents for at least 24 months for audit purposes</li>
</ul>
<h2>Financial Reconciliation Metrics: Ensuring Every Dollar Accounts For Itself</h2>
<p>Discovering mismatched accounts at month-end is one of the most frustrating issues for overseas marketing finance teams. Cross-verifying platform reports, bank statements, and channel data is the final step in fund management.</p>
<p><strong>Actionable Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Establish weekly reconciliation routines instead of handling everything at month-end—this reduces issue accumulation and investigation difficulty</li>
<li>[ ] Essential reconciliation items: top-up amount vs. received amount; platform-reported spend vs. bank debits; rebates/discounts correctly applied</li>
<li>[ ] Immediately investigate when variance rate (difference between top-up and spend divided by top-up amount) exceeds 2%</li>
<li>[ ] For enterprise account holders, verify tax and service fee line items on platform invoices</li>
<li>[ ] Account for time zones: Google and Meta reports default to account timezone—align with financial system timezone during reconciliation</li>
</ul>
<h2>Quick Reference Checklist Summary</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric Category</th>
<th>Core Checklist Items</th>
<th>Recommended Frequency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Account Health</td>
<td>Balance thresholds, top-up timing</td>
<td>Daily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend Efficiency</td>
<td>Daily spend ratio, CPM/CPA trends</td>
<td>Daily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cash Flow</td>
<td>Payment terms, currency buffers, top-up cadence</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Compliance &amp; Risk</td>
<td>Policy status, daily caps, IVT reports</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Financial Reconciliation</td>
<td>Three-way matching, variance rates, rebate verification</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Fund management is not solely the finance department's responsibility—it requires coordination among performance marketing, operations, and finance teams. We recommend monthly metric performance reviews to continuously optimize alert rules and checkpoint routines, ensuring your advertising budget delivers maximum impact.</p>
